%!PS-Adobe-2.0%%BeginProcSet "IWEM"%%  Imagewriter //  Emulator Version 1.1.2	4/27/87%    patterned after "DaisyEm.ps" by Adobe Systems%  Bill Judd  Apple Peripheral Systems and Products%  Copyright Apple Computer, Inc. 1986%  All Rights Reserved%---------------------------------------%   Modification  History%   5/31/86 wbj  First Release%   6/09/86 wbj  Strip off high bit for Apple // printing%   7/02/86 wbj  Fix graphic-printing bug%   --%%   7/10/86 Pete Mc Donald (PMM) adjusted Topofpage and LnHt, and Linect%                                to compensate for page only really being 10.92"%   7/12/86 PMM Add code to slash 0's (default in resetprinter else%               use set switch commands to change default)      %   7/17/86 PMM Add code to handle ignore/use bit 8. Default in Reset printer,%               else use set switch commands to change default)%   7/17/86 PMM Add code to handle Backspace.%   7/24/86 PMM Add code to do Hex Dumps of recieved data. Mode is controlled by%               a flag in reset printer, called debug? When true it will print%               all recieved characters as hexadecimal else it has no effect.%   7/25/86 PMM fix bug in ChangeSwitches, where certain operations were under%               the wrong switch number%	--%%   9/05/86 wbj fix blank-page-after-last-page bug%   9/11/86 wbj fix AW last-page bug%  10/04/86 wbj Let's go international! Imagewriter international character-%				switching function added%  11/03/86 wbj Added bdf proc: won some VM and some speed%  11/04/86 wbj modified Moustext generation code; moved Mousetext and%               graphic font generation to runtime%  11/05/86 wbj changed to table-lookup for internatonal char switching;%				cut # of fonts from 32 to 4%  11/07/86 wbj added procs to add AE and ae to Courier fonts%  11/10/86	wbj added subscript and superscript%  11/19/86 wbj headline-text function fixed%  11/25/86 wbj htabs problem fixed%  11/26/86 wbj international release today%  02/09/87 wbj fix NULL (ascii zero) bug today%  04/26/87 wbj fix graphic bug introduced with in't'l chars%% Modified by Wesley S. Johnson%%  01/22/88 WSJ	Replaced mirror code with table lookup code to fix %		(offending error caused by the idev command)%  01/26/88 WSJ	Put in RMtest to wrap-around very long strings of characters.%%  Imagewriter //  Emulator Version 1.2		1/27/88%%  04/06/88 WSJ changed Esc-S to use same entry point as Esc-G%		(takes care of Bitmap Graphics bug, discovered in Vers 1.2)%%  Imagewriter //  Emulator Version 1.2.1	4/18/88%%%  Imagewriter //  Emulator Version 1.3%%  d1%  Sept 23, 1988    Suki Lee       %  Added save in "initpage" and restore after showpage in "printfile" and%    "FF" (form feed) to recover the virtual memory used for each page. Also%    enhanced buildIWEm script to strip out unnecessary spaces around %  PostScript's special delimiter characters: (,),{,},[,],<,>,/,% to reduce%    file size by almost 1K bytes.%%  Sept 27, 1988    Suki Lee%  Defined xdf (exch def) and use whenever necessary. Substituted bdf where %    necessary, since this variable is already defined.%  Eliminated the procedure "inch" which was mainly used for defining margins.%    Hard coded the margin values in points and show the calculations in the %    comments since arithmetic calculations are costly in PostScript.%  Rewrote the Encoding vector definitions to be more efficient.%%  d2%  May 19, 1989     Suki Lee%  Added two new procs: oursave and ourrestore which uses the stack to transfer%    necessary variables across save and restore pairs. Modified FF to make use %    of these procs and makes a doFont call to keep font styles consistent across%    pages.%  Bug fix: In EscDict, 3 procs were defined using the incorrect decimal equivalent%    of the command: ESC- should be 45, not 44; ESC> should be 62, not 61; ESC? should%    be 63, not 62. Also the comment for ESC+ and ESC- are swapped.%  Added call to popCmdDict in ignore since all commands which use ignore needs to%    pop the dict off the stack. %%  May 20, 1989 	Suki Lee%  Bug fix: In EscDict, ESC g (103) is defined to use procedure gr3*4; but in the actual%    definition of the procedure, the name is defined as g3*4. Changed dict to use g3*4.%  Bug fix: Removed check for prop? in gf since that flag has effect only on text and not%    graphics. Graphics only need the dpi value. BRC# 45926%  Bug fix: Removed gsave and grestore in gprint. Grapics were being printed on top of%    each other since the current point is always restored to where the first graphic%    call begins. BRC #47016 & #48241(this reports the identical bug)%%  May 21, 1989     Suki Lee%  Change variable cpi to dpi to better reflect its purpose.%  Moved font set up from initjob to initpage. Moved call to initpage from initjob to %    printfile. This is done to maintain font consistency across pages.%%%  Imagewriter //  Emulator Version 1.4%%	June 5, 1991	Wes Johnson%	Re-Instated the "inch" procedure, since some values might not be known until runtime.%	New Feature:%		Added new Version checking code to allow later versions to overwrite currently%			installed IWEm.%		Added check for dostartpage.  If (dostartpage) showpage%	Bug fix:%		Added some variables which are used to set TopOfPage and LnHt during IWEm runtime.%		These variables will deal with the smaller imaging area of the Personal LaserWriter NT.%		Still allows 66 lines of text per page on a Personal LaserWriter NT.%		replaced some hard coded values with variables to reflect changes in imaging%			size of the Personal LaserWriter NT.%%	Feb 6, 1992	Wes Johnson%	Problem:%		Due to some changes in the IIgs the imaging area was reduced%		to allow grayscale to operate in its minimal configuration.%		This causes a side-effect of characters on the outside edges%		to be lost.%	Bug fix:%		When the IWEmDict is invoked it will execute the letter command.%		The letter command should cause most printers to default to the%		standard letter size imaging area.%	Note:%		This fix will not help the LaserWriter IIf with 2 meg (minimum config)%		The only solution is to upgrade it to 5 meg, then it will work like the IIg.%%    Version 1.5%	Feb 11, 1992	Greg Branche%		Changed version number to 1.5, since 1.4 was shipped with the%		LC //E Card software v2.0 without our knowledge.%%    Version 1.6d1%	Apr 24, 1992	Wes Johnson%		Changed version number to 1.6d1.  Changed check for Personal%		Laserwriter NT to also include the NTR (actually, and product%		with "Personal" in its name).  Changed copyright to include%		1992.%%   Version 1.6%	2-Apr-93 DAL%		Changed version to 1.6.%%   Spooler Version 1.6%	7-Apr-93 WSJ%		Removed startup page stuff & pre-amble which make IWEm dict permanent.%%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------/Ver (ImageWriter Emulator Version 1.6)def	% Note change /Version to match this string/bdf {bind def} bind def/xdf {exch def} bdf/IWEmDict 150 dict def/_WBJ_    {IWEmDict      begin     letter	% letter command should restore a normal letter imaging area     save	  makefonts      1 1 scale       printfile      restore  end} bdfIWEmDict begin %---------------------------------------------------------%% This Version string must coincide with "Ver" defined %/Version (ImageWriter Emulator Version 1.6)def	% Note change /Ver to match this string%% Personal LaserWriter NT support% These calculations only occur 1 time.% The values will be based on the result of the product string.%/inch 			{72 mul} bdf/PersonalLen		{10.63 inch} bdf/NormalLen		{10.75 inch} bdf/Personal?		{statusdict begin product end (Personal) search				{pop pop pop true} {pop false} ifelse} bdf	/ImageLen		{Personal? {PersonalLen}{NormalLen} ifelse} bdf/charsize		{Personal? {11.5}{11.7} ifelse} bdf% Setting the default language: you may change the default country by% choosing a country from the following list and replacing "USA" with% the chosen country in the command line below.  Do not include the "/".% To change the default to French characters, for example, change the% command line to: /defaultcountry France def% Then, when the IWEM is loaded into a Laserwriter printer, the % selected international characters will be the default characters.  % country list: DO NOT CHANGE !!!    /USA 0 def    /Italy 1 def    /Denmark 2 def    /England 3 def    /Germany 4 def    /Sweden 5 def    /France 6 def    /Spain 7 def/defaultcountry USA def		% <- this is the line to change/country defaultcountry def% ==============  MouseFont ====================/BuildCharDict 20 dict def/MouseFont 20 dict defMouseFont begin  /FontType 3 def  /FontMatrix [1 0 0 1 0 0] def  /FontBBox [0 0 1 1] def  /Encoding 256 array def 0 1 255 {Encoding exch /.notdef put} for/closedApple /openApple /arrow /hourGlass /check1 /check2 /invcrArrow /menubar /leftArrow /elipsis /downArrow /upArrow /overScore /crArrow /solidRect /lScrollArrow /rScrollArrow /dScrollArrow /uScrollArrow /midScore /lLeftCorner /rightArrow /grayRect1 /grayRect2 /lFolder /rFolder /rBar /diamond /horLines /cross /button /lBar  Encoding 64 32 getinterval astore pop /CharacterDefs 33 dict defCharacterDefs /.notdef {} putCharacterDefs /closedApple { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<08 10 6c fe fc fc 7e 6c>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /openApple { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<08 10 6c 82 84 84 52 6c>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /arrow { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<00 00 40 60 70 78 6c 42>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /hourGlass { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<fe 44 28 10 10 28 54 fe>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /check1 { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<00 02 04 88 50 20 20 00>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /check2 { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<00 02 04 88 50 20 20 00>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /invcrArrow { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<02 02 02 22 62 fe 60 20>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /menubar { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<ff 00 ff 00 ff 00 00 ff>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /leftArrow { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<10 20 40 ff 40 20 10 00>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /elipsis { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<00 00 00 00 00 00 00 54>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /downArrow { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<10 10 10 10 92 54 38 10>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /upArrow { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<10 38 54 92 10 10 10 10>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /overScore { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<ff 00 00 00 00 00 00 00>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /crArrow { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<02 02 02 22 62 fe 60 20>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /solidRect { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<ff ff ff ff ff ff ff ff>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /lScrollArrow { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<c9 18 38 7e 38 18 08 f7>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /rScrollArrow { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<93 18 1c fe 1c 18 10 ef>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /dScrollArrow { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<81 89 08 7f 3e 1c 89 81>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /uScrollArrow { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<81 89 1c 3e 7f 08 89 81>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /midScore { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<00 00 00 ff 00 00 00 00>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /lLeftCorner { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<80 80 80 80 80 80 80 ff>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /rightArrow { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<10 08 04 fe 04 08 10 00>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /grayRect1 { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<55 aa 55 aa 55 aa 55 aa>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /grayRect2 { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<55 aa 55 aa 55 aa 55 aa>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /lFolder { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<00 1f 21 20 20 20 3f 00>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /rFolder { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<00 00 f8 04 04 04 fc 00>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /rBar { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /diamond { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<10 38 7c fe 7c 38 10 00>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /horLines  { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<ff 00 00 00 00 00 00 ff>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /cross { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<28 28 ee 00 ee 28 28 00>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /button { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<fe 02 02 32 32 02 02 fe>} imagemask } putCharacterDefs /lBar { 8 8 true [ 8 0 0 -8 0 8] {<80 80 80 80 80 80 80 80>} imagemask } put/BuildChar  {BuildCharDict begin    /char xdf    /fontdict xdf    /charname fontdict /Encoding get char get def    /charproc fontdict /CharacterDefs get charname get def    1 0 0 0 1 1 setcachedevice    gsave charproc grestore  end } bdfend% ============== End of MouseFont Definition ================%@@@@@@@@@ Begin Old Mirror Code @@@@@@@@@@@@@%@/log2 128 def%@%@/mirror % n1 -- n2  reverse bits of 8-bit byte%@  { /log2 128 store%@	  0 exch					% -- 0 n%@		{ dup 2 mod 1 eq			% odd? -- 0 n f%@			{exch log2 add exch } if	% -- ans n%@			/log2 log2 2 idiv store%@			2 idiv%@			dup 0 eq { pop exit} if%@		} loop%@		1 idiv } def%@@@@@@@@@ End Old Mirror Code @@@@@@@@@@@@@@%################# Begin New Mirror Code ######################%%	Due to a problem in the LaserWriter versions 3.0+ the (Old mirror cmd) will not work any more%	it now offends the laser writer when an (idev) command is issued.%/Inverse	%Inverse Image of each character	[						%Begin Array% Inverse Images of 00-0F	0	% Inverse of 00000000	128	% Inverse of 00000001	64	% Inverse of 00000010	192	% Inverse of 00000011	32	% Inverse of 00000100	160	% Inverse of 00000101	96	% Inverse of 00000110	224	% Inverse of 00000111	16	% Inverse of 00001000	144	% Inverse of 00001001	80	% Inverse of 00001010	208	% Inverse of 00001011	48	% Inverse of 00001100	176	% Inverse of 00001101	112	% Inverse of 00001110	240	% Inverse of 00001111% Inverse Images of 10-1F	8	% Inverse of 00010000	136	% Inverse of 00010001	72	% Inverse of 00010010	200	% Inverse of 00010011	40	% Inverse of 00010100	168	% Inverse of 00010101	104	% Inverse of 00010110	232	% Inverse of 00010111	24	% Inverse of 00011000	152	% Inverse of 00011001	88	% Inverse of 00011010	216	% Inverse of 00011011	56	% Inverse of 00011100	184	% Inverse of 00011101	120	% Inverse of 00011110	248	% Inverse of 00011111% Inverse Images of 20-2F	4	% Inverse of 00100000	132	% Inverse of 00100001	68	% Inverse of 00100010	196	% Inverse of 00100011	36	% Inverse of 00100100	164	% Inverse of 00100101	100	% Inverse of 00100110	228	% Inverse of 00100111	20	% Inverse of 00101000	148	% Inverse of 00101001	84	% Inverse of 00101010	212	% Inverse of 00101011	52	% Inverse of 00101100	180	% Inverse of 00101101	116	% Inverse of 00101110	244	% Inverse of 00101111% Inverse Images of 30-3F	12	% Inverse of 00110000	140	% Inverse of 00110001	76	% Inverse of 00110010	204	% Inverse of 00110011	44	% Inverse of 00110100	172	% Inverse of 00110101	108	% Inverse of 00110110	236	% Inverse of 00110111	28	% Inverse of 00111000	156	% Inverse of 00111001	92	% Inverse of 00111010	220	% Inverse of 00111011	60	% Inverse of 00111100	188	% Inverse of 00111101	124	% Inverse of 00111110	252	% Inverse of 00111111% Inverse Images of 40-4F	2	% Inverse of 01000000	130	% Inverse of 01000001	66	% Inverse of 01000010	194	% Inverse of 01000011	34	% Inverse of 01000100	162	% Inverse of 01000101	98	% Inverse of 01000110	226	% Inverse of 01000111	18	% Inverse of 01001000	146	% Inverse of 01001001	82	% Inverse of 01001010	210	% Inverse of 01001011	50	% Inverse of 01001100	178	% Inverse of 01001101	114	% Inverse of 01001110	242	% Inverse of 01001111% Inverse Images of 50-5F	10	% Inverse of 01010000	138	% Inverse of 01010001	74	% Inverse of 01010010	202	% Inverse of 01010011	42	% Inverse of 01010100	170	% Inverse of 01010101	106	% Inverse of 01010110	234	% Inverse of 01010111	26	% Inverse of 01011000	154	% Inverse of 01011001	90	% Inverse of 01011010	218	% Inverse of 01011011	58	% Inverse of 01011100	186	% Inverse of 01011101	122	% Inverse of 01011110	250	% Inverse of 01011111% Inverse Images of 60-6F	6	% Inverse of 01100000	134	% Inverse of 01100001	70	% Inverse of 01100010	198	% Inverse of 01100011	38	% Inverse of 01100100	166	% Inverse of 01100101	102	% Inverse of 01100110	230	% Inverse of 01100111	22	% Inverse of 01101000	150	% Inverse of 01101001	86	% Inverse of 01101010	214	% Inverse of 01101011	54	% Inverse of 01101100	182	% Inverse of 01101101	118	% Inverse of 01101110	246	% Inverse of 01101111% Inverse Images of 70-7F	14	% Inverse of 01110000	142	% Inverse of 01110001	78	% Inverse of 01110010	206	% Inverse of 01110011	46	% Inverse of 01110100	174	% Inverse of 01110101	110	% Inverse of 01110110	238	% Inverse of 01110111	30	% Inverse of 01111000	158	% Inverse of 01111001	94	% Inverse of 01111010	222	% Inverse of 01111011	62	% Inverse of 01111100	190	% Inverse of 01111101	126	% Inverse of 01111110	254	% Inverse of 01111111% Inverse Images of 80-8F	1	% Inverse of 10000000	129	% Inverse of 10000001	65	% Inverse of 10000010	193	% Inverse of 10000011	33	% Inverse of 10000100	161	% Inverse of 10000101	97	% Inverse of 10000110	225	% Inverse of 10000111	17	% Inverse of 10001000	145	% Inverse of 10001001	81	% Inverse of 10001010	209	% Inverse of 10001011	49	% Inverse of 10001100	177	% Inverse of 10001101	113	% Inverse of 10001110	241	% Inverse of 10001111% Inverse Images of 90-9F	9	% Inverse of 10010000	137	% Inverse of 10010001	73	% Inverse of 10010010	201	% Inverse of 10010011	41	% Inverse of 10010100	169	% Inverse of 10010101	105	% Inverse of 10010110	233	% Inverse of 10010111	25	% Inverse of 10011000	153	% Inverse of 10011001	89	% Inverse of 10011010	217	% Inverse of 10011011	57	% Inverse of 10011100	185	% Inverse of 10011101	121	% Inverse of 10011110	249	% Inverse of 10011111% Inverse